    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The libertarian stance on taxation is rooted in the philosophical ideas of individual rights and the concept of 'self-ownership.' Adam Smith, in \"The Theory of Moral Sentiments,\" and later John Locke argue that individuals are the rightful owners of their own labor and property, and that their freedom to make choices about how to allocate resources is compromised when taxes are imposed without their consent. \n\nThe libertarian philosopher John Courtney Murray, drawing on the works of Robert Nozick, justifies the state's role in taxation through the concept of 'entitlement theory,' which holds that individuals have rights to the products of their labor. According to this view, taxation infringes upon these individual rights, as it takes resources away from individuals without their consent. This justification emphasizes the moral gravity of economic transactions, as it connects taxation to forced labor and enslavement, rather than simply a matter of personal financial choice.",
      "source": "target",
